---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

/*
  This page used to be a renewal checkout: drop your license file, see the window
  you'd get, pay. Updates are permanent now, so there is nothing to sell here.

  It is NOT deleted, and the URL must not 404. Sealshot 0.7.8 and earlier ship a
  "Renew License…" button in Settings ▸ License that opens
  /renew/?license_id=…&email=…, and a link compiled into a binary cannot be taken
  back — someone will click it years from now. The FAQ and the refunds page point
  here too. So the page answers the question the button was asking.

  The license-file verification, the /renew/verify lookup and the volume-license
  branch all went with the checkout. The Worker endpoint stays (it is tested and
  harmless) in case a lookup surface is wanted again.
*/
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Renewing your Sealshot license — Sealshot"
  description="Nothing to renew: a Sealshot license now includes every future update, permanently."
>
  <section class="section wrap prose" aria-labelledby="renew-title">
    <p class="kicker">Renew</p>
    <h1 id="renew-title">There's nothing to renew</h1>
    <p class="sub">
      A Sealshot license includes <strong>every future update, permanently</strong>.
      No window, no expiry, and no renewal to remember — if you have a license,
      you are done.
    </p>

    <h2>If you bought before this changed</h2>
    <p>
      Licenses issued up to August 2026 covered a fixed number of months of
      updates. Those are being replaced with permanent ones at no cost — you are
      not being asked to pay for what you already bought. Email
      <a href="mailto:support@seal-shot.com">support@seal-shot.com</a> with the
      <strong>License ID</strong> from <strong>Settings ▸ License</strong> and a
      replacement file will come back to you.
    </p>

    <h2>How to tell which one you have</h2>
    <p>
      Open your <code>.sealshotlicense</code> file in any text editor, or look at
      <strong>Settings ▸ License</strong> in Sealshot. A permanent license reads
      <em>Updates — All future versions</em>. An older one names a date it runs
      through.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens if you do nothing</h2>
    <p>
      Sealshot keeps working, and so does every version your license already
      covers. Newer versions install and run in full too — the app is
      <a href="/buy/">free to use</a>, so a version outside an old window is
      never a lockout. The only difference is that the occasional support
      reminder comes back, and a replacement license turns it off again.
    </p>

    <p class="fine-print">
      Volume licenses are handled the same way — reply to your invoice or email
      <a href="mailto:support@seal-shot.com">support@seal-shot.com</a> with the
      license ID, and every seat is replaced at once.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
